50: $6,270 a year, class C</title></rect><text transform=\"rotate(-45 523.9 344)\" x=\"523.9\" y=\"344\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#5A6475\">NS-116</text><rect x=\"538.8\" y=\"326.3\" width=\"21.8\" height=\"3.7\" fill=\"#D3D9E2\"><title>NS-117 Floor marking tape, roll: $5,280 a year, class C</title></rect><text transform=\"rotate(-45 553.7 344)\" x=\"553.7\" y=\"344\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#5A6475\">NS-117</text><rect x=\"568.6\" y=\"327.0\" width=\"21.8\" height=\"3.0\" fill=\"#D3D9E2\"><title>NS-118 Zip ties, bag of 1,000: $4,320 a year, class C</title></rect><text transform=\"rotate(-45 583.5 344)\" x=\"583.5\" y=\"344\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#5A6475\">NS-118</text><rect x=\"598.4\" y=\"327.9\" width=\"21.8\" height=\"2.1\" fill=\"#D3D9E2\"><title>NS-119 Spray bottles, case of 12: $2,970 a year, class C</title></rect><text transform=\"rotate(-45 613.3 344)\" x=\"613.3\" y=\"344\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#5A6475\">NS-119</text><rect x=\"628.2\" y=\"328.0\" width=\"21.8\" height=\"2.0\" fill=\"#D3D9E2\"><title>NS-120 Replacement blades, pack of 100: $1,710 a year, class C</title></rect><text transform=\"rotate(-45 643.1 344)\" x=\"643.1\" y=\"344\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-size=\"10\" fill=\"#5A6475\">NS-120</text><line x1=\"58\" x2=\"654\" y1=\"330\" y2=\"330\" stroke=\"#8A93A3\"/><text x=\"147.4\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\">A: 6 items, 78.9%</text><line x1=\"236.8\" x2=\"236.8\" y1=\"98\" y2=\"330\" stroke=\"#8A93A3\"/><text x=\"242.8\" y=\"173.7\" fill=\"#26324A\">78.9%</text><text x=\"296.4\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\">B: 4 items, 15.6%</text><line x1=\"356.0\" x2=\"356.0\" y1=\"98\" y2=\"330\" stroke=\"#8A93A3\"/><text x=\"362.0\" y=\"138.4\" fill=\"#26324A\">94.5%</text><text x=\"505.0\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\">C: 10 items, 5.5%</text><polyline points=\"72.9,278.4 102.7,240.0 132.5,206.9 162.3,180.1 192.1,164.3 221.9,151.7 251.7,140.9 281.5,131.1 311.3,122.4 341.1,116.4 370.9,112.8 400.7,111.0 430.5,109.7 460.3,108.5 490.1,107.3 519.9,106.3 549.7,105.4 579.5,104.7 609.3,104.3 639.1,104.0\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#0B2A5A\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><circle cx=\"72.9\" cy=\"278.4\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-101: cumulative 22.8%</title></circle><circle cx=\"102.7\" cy=\"240.0\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-102: cumulative 39.8%</title></circle><circle cx=\"132.5\" cy=\"206.9\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-103: cumulative 54.5%</title></circle><circle cx=\"162.3\" cy=\"180.1\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-104: cumulative 66.3%</title></circle><circle cx=\"192.1\" cy=\"164.3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-105: cumulative 73.3%</title></circle><circle cx=\"221.9\" cy=\"151.7\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-106: cumulative 78.9%</title></circle><circle cx=\"251.7\" cy=\"140.9\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-107: cumulative 83.7%</title></circle><circle cx=\"281.5\" cy=\"131.1\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-108: cumulative 88.0%</title></circle><circle cx=\"311.3\" cy=\"122.4\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-109: cumulative 91.9%</title></circle><circle cx=\"341.1\" cy=\"116.4\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-110: cumulative 94.5%</title></circle><circle cx=\"370.9\" cy=\"112.8\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-111: cumulative 96.1%</title></circle><circle cx=\"400.7\" cy=\"111.0\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-112: cumulative 96.9%</title></circle><circle cx=\"430.5\" cy=\"109.7\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-113: cumulative 97.5%</title></circle><circle cx=\"460.3\" cy=\"108.5\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-114: cumulative 98.0%</title></circle><circle cx=\"490.1\" cy=\"107.3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-115: cumulative 98.5%</title></circle><circle cx=\"519.9\" cy=\"106.3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-116: cumulative 99.0%</title></circle><circle cx=\"549.7\" cy=\"105.4\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-117: cumulative 99.4%</title></circle><circle cx=\"579.5\" cy=\"104.7\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-118: cumulative 99.7%</title></circle><circle cx=\"609.3\" cy=\"104.3\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-119: cumulative 99.9%</title></circle><circle cx=\"639.1\" cy=\"104.0\" r=\"2.5\" fill=\"#0B2A5A\"><title>NS-120: cumulative 100.0%</title></circle></svg><figcaption>Illustrative data for a fictional distributor: 20 SKUs, 12 months of usage.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Three things stand out:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Six A items carry $1,114,620, or 78.9% of the total.</strong> These are 30% of the items. A 5% ordering error on gloves alone is worth more than the entire yearly usage of the bottom four items combined.</li>\n<li><strong>Four B items carry $220,690, or 15.6%.</strong> They matter, but a standard reorder routine is enough.</li>\n<li><strong>Ten C items carry $77,690, or 5.5%.</strong> Half the item list, and half the counting and ordering effort, is spent on one-twentieth of the value.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notice that rank and unit cost are not the same thing. The pallet strapping kit is the most expensive item at $165, yet it lands in class B. Stretch wrap costs $24.50 and ranks second, because volume counts as much as price.</p>\n<h2>What to do with each class</h2>\n<p>The classes only pay off when each one gets a different level of control. The table below is a practical starting policy.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th style=\"white-space:nowrap\">Practice</th><th style=\"white-space:nowrap\">A items</th><th style=\"white-space:nowrap\">B items</th><th style=\"white-space:nowrap\">C items</th></tr>\n<tr><td style=\"white-space:nowrap\">Cycle count</td><td style=\"white-space:nowrap\">Monthly</td><td style=\"white-space:nowrap\">Quarterly</td><td style=\"white-space:nowrap\">Once or twice a year</td></tr>\n<tr><td style=\"white-space:nowrap\">Reorder review</td><td style=\"white-space:nowrap\">Weekly</td><td>Every two weeks or monthly</td><td>Quarterly, or only when the minimum is hit</td></tr>\n<tr><td style=\"white-space:nowrap\">Ordering method</td><td>Forecast-based, smaller and more frequent orders</td><td>Reorder point with a standard order quantity</td><td>Simple min/max or two-bin, larger and less frequent orders</td></tr>\n<tr><td style=\"white-space:nowrap\">Supplier attention</td><td>Negotiated pricing, a backup source, lead times tracked</td><td>Standard terms, reviewed yearly</td><td>Consolidate with one supplier to cut paperwork</td></tr>\n<tr><td style=\"white-space:nowrap\">Who decides</td><td>Buyer or manager reviews each order</td><td>Buyer approves system suggestions</td><td>Automatic, or delegated to the warehouse</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The logic is simple. A items justify a person's attention because a small error costs real money. C items do not, so the cheapest control that prevents stockouts is the right one. Holding a little extra of a C item costs almost nothing; spending an hour a week managing it costs more than the item is worth.</p>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<p><strong>Classifying on dollar value alone.</strong> Some low-value items are critical. If one customer buys their A items from you only because you also stock a particular C item, running out of it costs far more than its ranking suggests. Review the C list by hand and flag the exceptions.</p>\n<p><strong>Running it once.</strong> Products rise and fade, costs change, and customers come and go. Re-run the analysis at least once a year, and every six months if your range changes quickly.</p>\n<p><strong>Using too short a period.</strong> Three months of data will misclassify anything seasonal. Use 12 months, and for new items use a forecast until they have a history.</p>\n<p><strong>Changing the labels but not the behaviour.</strong> The analysis is worth nothing if every item is still counted and reordered the same way afterwards. Write the class policies down and build them into the routine.</p>\n<p><strong>Ignoring how steady the demand is.</strong> Two A items with the same annual value can behave very differently. One sells the same amount every week; the other swings between nothing and a truckload. ABC tells you which items matter. It does not tell you how much buffer each one needs. That depends on demand variability and supplier lead time, which is the subject of <a href=\"setting-safety-stock.html\">the next post</a> on safety stock.</p>\n<h2>Your checklist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Export 12 months of usage and current unit cost for every stocked item</li>\n<li>Calculate annual dollar usage and rank the list</li>\n<li>Add cumulative percentages and assign A, B and C classes</li>\n<li>Review the C list by hand for critical items</li>\n<li>Write a count, review and ordering policy for each class</li>\n<li>Put a date in the calendar to re-run the analysis</li>\n</ul>\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"../assets/css/download.css\"><div class=\"j-dl-box\"><div><strong>Free Excel template</strong><span>A workbook accompanies this series. It ranks and classifies your items automatically and includes the example data from this post.</span></div><a class=\"btn\" href=\"../assets/downloads/Chmltech_Inventory_ABC_SafetyStock_Template.xlsx\" data-dl download>Download template</a></div>\n<p><strong>Next in the series:</strong> <a href=\"setting-safety-stock.html\">Post&nbsp;2</a> takes three items from this example and sets a safety stock and reorder point for each.</p>\n<p><em>Chmltech Ltd. helps small and mid-sized businesses improve operations and adopt digital tools. If you would like help applying this to your own inventory, <a href=\"../contact.html\">get in touch</a>.</em></p>\n<dialog class=\"j-dialog\" id=\"dl\" aria-labelledby=\"dl-title\" data-file=\"../assets/downloads/Chmltech_Inventory_ABC_SafetyStock_Template.xlsx\" data-fname=\"Chmltech_Inventory_ABC_SafetyStock_Template.xlsx\"